theory.intro:
A **tree** is a connected network with no loops, multiple edges, or cycles. A **spanning tree** connects all $n$ vertices of a graph using $n - 1$ edges.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.2,3.0);
\coordinate (D) at (6.0,1.5);
\coordinate (E) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (F) at (1.8,0);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (A) -- (B);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (B) -- (C);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (C) -- (D);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (D) -- (E);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (F) -- (A);
\draw (B) -- (F);
\draw (C) -- (E);
\draw (B) -- (E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$F$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Consider the network diagram below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3.5);
\coordinate (C) at (4,2);
\coordinate (D) at (1,0.5);
\coordinate (E) at (3,0.5);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (A) -- (D);
\draw (C) -- (E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State whether this graph is a tree and justify your answer.

development.question_text:
The diagram shows a connected network with $6$ vertices.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3.5);
\coordinate (C) at (4,3.5);
\coordinate (D) at (6,2);
\coordinate (E) at (4,0.5);
\coordinate (F) at (2,0.5);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\draw (F) -- (A);
\draw (B) -- (F);
\draw (C) -- (E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$F$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Calculate the number of edges that must be removed to construct a spanning tree.

The network diagram shows $5$ vertices $A, B, C, D, E$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3.5);
\coordinate (C) at (4,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3,0);
\coordinate (E) at (1,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (D);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Candidate Subgraph 1 has edges $\{AB, BC, CD, EA\}$. Candidate Subgraph 2 has edges $\{AB, BC, CA, DE\}$.
Determine which candidate is a valid spanning tree and explain why the other fails.

